{{smaller block|And thus we Sayled ''thorow'' the Gulf of Seynt Elene, otherwyse callyd the Gulf of Satalie, And com a long the Costes of Turkey, And ther we saw the Mowntaynes of Macedonye. {{right|''Torkington'', Diarie of Eng. Travell, p. 57.}}}}

{{smaller block|Over hill, over dale,
''Thorough'' bush, ''thorough'' brier,
Over park, over pale,
''Thorough'' flood, ''thorough'' fire.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., ii. 1. 3, 5.}}}}

'''II.''' ''adv.'' Through: as, ''thorough''going. See ''through''<sup>1</sup>, ''adv.''

'''thorough''' (thur′ō), ''a.'' [< ''thorough'', ''adv.''] '''1.''' Going through; through, in a literal sense: a form now occurring only in dialectal use or in certain phrases and compounds. See ''through''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''

{{smaller block|Let all three sides be a double house, without ''thorough'' lights on the sides. {{right|''Bacon'', Building (ed. 1887).}}}}

'''2.''' Going through, as to the end or bottom of anything; thoroughgoing. {{smaller|Hence—(''a'') Penetrating; searching; sharp; keen.}}

{{smaller block|The intuitive decision of a bright
And ''thorough''-edged intellect to part
Error from crime. {{right|''Tennyson'', Isabel.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') Leaving nothing undone; slighting nothing; not superficial.}}

{{smaller block|To be a ''thorough'' translator, he must be a ''thorough'' poet. {{right|''Dryden'', Translation.}}}}

{{smaller|(''c'') Fully executed; having no deficiencies; hence, complete in all respects; unqualified; perfect.}}

{{smaller block|Me seemes the Irish Horse-boyes or Cuilles . . . in the ''thorough'' reformation of that realme . . . should be cutt of. {{right|''Spenser'', State of Ireland}}}}

{{smaller block|Dark night,
Strike a full silence, do a ''thorow'' right
To this great chorus.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Maid's Tragedy, i.}}}}

