{{smaller block|A ''thorough'' discussion of the evils and dangers of all paper money, by whomsoever issued.
{{right|''The Nation'', XXI. 112.}}}}

{{smaller|(''d'') Earnest; ardent. [Rare.]}}

{{smaller block|She 's taen him in her arms twa,
And gien him kisses ''thorough''.
{{right|''The Braes o' Yarrow'' (Child's Ballads, III. 71).}}}}

{{smaller|'''Thorough framing†''', the framing of doors and windows.—'''Thorough stress.''' See ''stress''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Toll thorough.''' See ''toll''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''thorough''' (thur′ō), ''n.'' [< ''thorough'', ''a.'' or ''adv.''] 1. That which goes through. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a''†) A thoroughfare; a passage; a channel.}}

{{smaller block|If any man would alter the natural course of any water to run a contrary way, . . . the alteration must be from the head, by making other ''thoroughs'' and devices.
{{right|''J. Bradford'', Works (Parker Soc.), I. 303. (''Davies.'')}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') A furrow between two ridges. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.] (''c'') Same as ''perpend''<sup>3</sup>.}}

2. In ''Brit. hist.'', in the reign of Charles I., the policy of Strafford and Laud of conducting or carrying through ('thorough') the administration of public affairs without regard to obstacles. Hence the word is associated with their system of tyranny.

{{smaller block|The dark, gloomy countenance, the full, heavy eye, which meet us in Strafford's portrait, are the best commentary on his policy of ''Thorough''.
{{right|''J. R. Green'', Short Hist. Eng., p. 509.}}}}

